WEBVTT

00:00:08,880 --> 00:00:18,480
One, if my cancer metastasizes to my spine, do I need an update on my life frequencies.

00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:23,440
So, remember, no, if your cancer metastasizes, it's still cancer.

00:00:23,440 --> 00:00:25,280
So, let's say I have a breast cancer.

00:00:25,280 --> 00:00:29,200
And now I found out it's metastasized to my thoracic vertebrae.

00:00:29,840 --> 00:00:32,880
Oh my gosh, do I need a bone cancer programmed out?

00:00:32,880 --> 00:00:33,600
No, you don't.

00:00:34,320 --> 00:00:37,200
It's still breast cancer in the bone.

00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:42,160
So, if your original primary cancer metastasizes to another organ,

00:00:42,160 --> 00:00:45,200
it still sells from that original cancer.

00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:48,560
So, if I have prostate cancer and it metastasizes to my liver,

00:00:48,560 --> 00:00:52,000
it's not liver cancer now, it's still prostate cancer in my liver.

00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:55,200
So, those same frequencies will be helpful.

00:00:55,760 --> 00:01:00,320
So, is there ever a reason why I should update my cancer program?

00:01:00,560 --> 00:01:08,080
Maybe, but not one that I can really think of.

00:01:08,080 --> 00:01:12,400
Well, I do have some people that have a second cancer,

00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:14,880
a second primary cancer that they found out.

00:01:15,680 --> 00:01:20,320
They had abdominal cancer, colon cancer that they found out.

00:01:21,040 --> 00:01:24,240
They had some spots that were lung that were biopsy,

00:01:24,240 --> 00:01:26,400
and it was a primary lung cancer.

00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:29,360
So, that's not great news.

00:01:29,360 --> 00:01:31,600
You got two completely different cancers.

00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:36,720
That's a reason to update your frequencies because I have to put 50% of the night

00:01:36,720 --> 00:01:40,880
in colon cancer and 50% of the night in lung cancer.

00:01:41,760 --> 00:01:44,320
Because that's a total, those are different frequencies.

00:01:44,320 --> 00:01:46,240
So, that would be a reason.

00:01:46,240 --> 00:01:49,680
But if I have a primary cancer that metastasizes the bone,

00:01:49,680 --> 00:01:51,520
it's going to be the same frequencies.

00:01:52,320 --> 00:01:56,400
So, it's always good to ask the question.

00:01:56,400 --> 00:02:00,320
So, if you have that question on your cheek swab test,

00:02:00,320 --> 00:02:02,560
check for, is there any new frequencies?

00:02:04,080 --> 00:02:05,360
There have been over the year.

00:02:05,360 --> 00:02:08,000
So, I would update your program.

00:02:09,280 --> 00:02:11,360
So, I'll let you know if we need to do that.

00:02:11,360 --> 00:02:13,520
What you said in your cheek swab test.

